  "answer": "<p>The correct choice is <b>Use Cloud Storage for intake, Cloud Pub/Sub to fan out processing jobs, Cloud Dataflow to handle video transcoding and watermark overlay, and Video Intelligence API to extract labels for search</b>.</p><p>This design is serverless and scales automatically from a few small files to hundreds of large assets. Cloud Storage provides durable intake and events can publish to Pub/Sub to decouple upload from processing and to fan out work. Dataflow runs managed workers that auto scale with backlog and can execute custom steps such as invoking FFmpeg for transcoding and watermark overlay while you only manage pipeline code and not servers. Video Intelligence API adds labels and other metadata so content can be indexed and searched. The result minimizes operations while handling unpredictable spikes.</p><p><i>Use Cloud Functions to react to new uploads in Cloud Storage, process videos with custom FFmpeg containers on Cloud Run for transcoding and watermarking, and index metadata in Elasticsearch running on Compute Engine</i> is not the best fit because running Elasticsearch on Compute Engine adds significant operational overhead and capacity management. Long running or heavy video jobs do not fit well in a function trigger and would need additional orchestration to reliably handle large files and bursts.</p><p><i>Use Cloud Storage for uploads, Cloud Video Intelligence API for searchable metadata, and Compute Engine managed instance groups for transcoding and watermarking</i> is not serverless because managed instance groups still require VM provisioning, scaling policies, patching, and monitoring. The question asks to minimize operations and to scale automatically without managing servers.</p><p><i>Implement Cloud Dataprep for video processing, use Cloud Vision API for watermark tasks, and load metadata to BigQuery for search</i> is incorrect because Dataprep is for data wrangling of tabular and semi structured data and is not suited for video pipelines and it has been retired from Google Cloud which makes it even less likely on newer exams. Vision API targets images and does not perform video watermarking. While BigQuery can store metadata, this does not address the core transcoding and watermark workflow.</p>",
